> METADATA can contain
  > branch   history/target/prediction_strength
  > indirect call history/target/prediction_strength
  > data access history/prediction_strength [1]

> The processor should detect and patch this data directly in the i-cache
  So yes, i-cache will have to be writeback and processor writeable

> If you look careful, this is very similar to JITs except code perf and history
  is getting updated and speculated very fast without runtime SW initiated code
  patching.

> Of course METADATASIZE can be 0 if compiler so desires	

[1] this information can be used to do prefetch and speculative coherence
    i.e., pull cachelines in and pay for coherence and cache misses upfront

